  <h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
  <p>When downloading videos from the internet, you'll often be presented with a choice of formats. The two most common options are <strong>MP4</strong> and <strong>WebM</strong>. Both are excellent, modern video container formats, but they serve slightly different purposes and have different levels of compatibility.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is-webm">What is WebM?</h2>
  <p>While MP4 is a universal standard with roots in older MPEG formats, <strong>WebM</strong> is a relatively newer format created by Google. It was designed specifically for the web—it's open-source, royalty-free, and highly optimized for streaming HTML5 video.</p>
  <p>WebM files typically use VP8 or VP9 video codecs and Vorbis or Opus audio codecs. These are highly efficient compression technologies that deliver great quality at smaller file sizes compared to older codecs.</p>

  <h2 id="comparison">Feature Comparison</h2>
  
  <ComparisonTable 
